### Step 1: Write the reaction and calculate molar mass of phenol

Molar mass of phenol (\( C_{6}H_{5}OH \)) = \( (6 \times 12) + (6 \times 1) + (1 \times 16) = 72 + 6 + 16 = 94 \text{ g/mol} \)

### Step 2: Calculate the initial moles of phenol

\text{Moles of phenol} = \frac{\text{Mass of phenol}}{\text{Molar mass of phenol}} = \frac{4.7 \text{ g}}{94.0 \text{ g/mol}} = 0.05 \text{ mol}

### Step 3: Determine the theoretical moles of product X (Benzene)

From the balanced reaction, 1 mole of phenol gives 1 mole of benzene. Therefore, 0.05 mole of phenol will theoretically yield 0.05 mole of benzene (product X) at 100% completion.

### Step 4: Calculate the actual moles of product X formed at 60% completion

\text{Actual moles of X} = \text{Theoretical moles of X} \times \text{Yield percentage}
\text{Actual moles of X} = 0.05 \text{ mol} \times 0.60 = 0.03 \text{ mol}

### Step 5: Express the answer in the required format

The number of moles of product X formed is \( 0.03 \text{ mol} \). To express this in the format \( \times 10^{-2} \), we write:
0.03 \text{ mol} = 3 \times 10^{-2} \text{ mol}
The nearest integer value is 3.
